# micro-identity
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env.
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE DID NOT EXIST UNTIL NOW ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every other service in the estate ships one, and identity — the service every
# other service authenticates against — did not. The absence was found by
# deploying it: the first vertical slice
# (`deploy/compose/docker-compose.slice.yml`) had to reconstruct this list by
# reading `src/env.ts`, and one value was guessed wrong, at which point identity
# refused to start and named every scope it knows.
#
# That refusal is the design working. But a service whose configuration can only
# be learned by reading its source is a service nobody can deploy without reading
# its source, and the estate's own rule — 03 §2 rule 9, "a repo declares the
# variables it needs; the deploy provides exactly those" — is not satisfied by a
# declaration that exists only in TypeScript.
#
# Every variable below is one `src/env.ts` actually reads. The defaults are the
# ones it actually applies.

# ---- service ----------------------------------------------------------------
PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev
INSTANCE_ID=identity-local

# ---- database ---------------------------------------------------------------
IDENTITY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://cloudsforge:CHANGE_ME@127.0.0.1:5432/identity
IDENTITY_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# ---- identity of this issuer -------------------------------------------------
# `IDENTITY_ISSUER` is the `iss` claim every service checks, and
# `IDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL` is where its JWKS is fetched from. They are separate
# because an issuer is a NAME and a public URL is an ADDRESS: a token issued
# before a hostname change must keep verifying afterwards.
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://localhost:4000
IDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:4000

# ---- secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FORGING KEY. `src/env.ts` calls this "the estate's universal forging
# credential": whoever holds it can mint a token for any user and any service,
# and every service in the estate will accept it. It is not a password — it is
# the root of trust.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# WHAT THE VALUE MAY BE, ENFORCED AT BOOT BY `assertGeneratedSecret`
# (@cloudsforge/secrets, called from src/env.ts). There is no way to turn this
# off — no NODE_ENV branch, no IDENTITY_ALLOW_* variable — and that is the whole
# point of it:
#
#   * base64 or hex, and NOTHING ELSE. A hyphen or an underscore is what a human
#     types and a generator never emits, so a typed value cannot be one.
#   * at least 32 BYTES of key material — not 32 characters. The line below used
#     to read `CHANGE_ME_at_least_32_characters_long`, and IT BOOTED: 37
#     characters, past the floor, and not one of the exact strings on the
#     deny-list. Characters were never the unit, and a count of them is what let
#     a 40-character placeholder reach 44 containers on both networks (#142).
#   * enough measured entropy that a long degenerate value — 64 zeros,
#     `deadbeef` eight times — is refused as well.
#
# The value below still says CHANGE_ME, deliberately: this file must ship
# something that CANNOT boot, because a secret committed here is a secret in
# every clone of this repository. `env.test.ts` asserts both halves — that the
# shipped file refuses to load, and that it loads once the two placeholders are
# replaced with real values.
IDENTITY_KEY_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# Signs the outbox envelope, and verifies every inbound one. Must match what
# consumers verify with. Held to exactly the same rule as the key above — this
# line used to say `at_least_24_characters`, which was two things wrong at once.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# ---- service tokens ----------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH SERVICE MAY HOLD WHICH SCOPE, as JSON. The scope names are validated at
# import against identity's own SCOPE_NAMES, and an unknown one is a refusal to
# start that lists every scope it knows — which is how the first slice found it
# had invented `ledger:write`.
#
# Empty by default, deliberately: a service grant is an authorisation decision,
# and defaulting one would be granting it.
IDENTITY_SERVICE_TOKEN_GRANTS={}

# ---- sign-in handoff ----------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONLY OPEN-REDIRECT GUARD IN THE ESTATE'S SSO. Comma-separated origins that
# may receive a session hand-off.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS IF IT IS WRONG, both ways:
#
#   Too NARROW (or empty) — `createHandoffCode` refuses every origin not on it
#   and mints nothing (`src/handoff.ts:31-58`). A user signs in at Hub and then
#   cannot reach any other surface: hub-web shows "CloudsForge will not hand a
#   session to <origin>" rather than bouncing them somewhere, precisely so the
#   misconfiguration is visible instead of looking like a broken product
#   (`hub-web/src/lib/identity.ts:229-232`).
#
#   Too WIDE — every origin on this list can be handed a live session for
#   whoever just signed in. `?return=` is a query parameter on a public page, so
#   it is attacker-controlled, and hub-web deliberately keeps NO second list of
#   its own ("a second list is a list that drifts", identity.ts:255-263). One
#   wrong entry here is not a loose CORS header; it is token delivery to
#   somebody else's page. Never a wildcard, never an origin the platform does
#   not serve, never `http://` off loopback.
#
# The DEFAULT IN CODE IS EMPTY AND STAYS EMPTY (`src/env.ts:219-223`, pinned by
# `a complete source loads` in src/env.test.ts). An unset variable costs
# cross-surface sign-in; it must never cost the guard. So this file carries a
# working value rather than an empty one — an example that boots an estate where
# every hand-off is refused documents nothing.
#
# The value below is the LOCAL `pnpm dev` set, read from the surface registry
# (`ui/packages/ui/src/surfaces.ts` — `http://localhost:<devPort>` per row, which
# is what `cloudsforgeHosts()` resolves to on localhost). Not a guess: the
# registry exists because this same list was once hand-maintained in eight places
# and had already drifted. A deployment behind the gateway replaces it with
# `https://<subdomain>.<apex>` for the same surfaces.
#
# Hub (3010) is on the list even though `completeSignIn` short-circuits a
# same-origin return without minting anything: the origin that mints already
# holds the session, so listing it grants no capability it lacks, and leaving it
# off couples this file to that short-circuit for no reduction in risk.
#
# `foresight-admin` has no registry row and therefore no port to put here; it is
# recorded as a gap for micro-ui in deploy/compose/docker-compose.estate.yml.
IDENTITY_HANDOFF_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3001,http://localhost:3003,http://localhost:3010,http://localhost:3012,http://localhost:3013,http://localhost:4004,http://localhost:4006,http://localhost:4007,http://localhost:4008,http://localhost:4014,http://localhost:4021,http://localhost:4100,http://localhost:4120

# ---- where the account pages live -----------------------------------------------
# The origin of the page an email-verification link points at. NOT this service:
# the link opens `<IDENTITY_ACCOUNT_URL>/account/verify#token=…`, which Hub serves,
# and that page posts the token back to `POST /auth/email/verify` here.
#
# OPTIONAL, and the only optional URL in this file. Unset is a supported mode: the
# token is still minted and `identity.email.verification_requested` is still
# emitted, carrying `linkable: false` instead of a `verifyUrl`, so registration
# keeps working in an estate that has not wired Hub yet. What it costs is the
# button in the email, which is why a deployment that sends mail must set it.
#
# 3010 is Hub's local `pnpm dev` port — the same row of the surface registry the
# hand-off list above takes it from. A deployment behind the gateway uses
# `https://hub.<apex>`.
IDENTITY_ACCOUNT_URL=http://localhost:3010

# ---- account deletion ----------------------------------------------------------
# Days between a deletion request and erasure, during which it can be cancelled.
IDENTITY_DELETION_GRACE_DAYS=7

# ---- the registration challenge (Cloudflare Turnstile) --------------------------
# COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE, AND THE COMMENTING IS THE DECLARATION. All three are
# optional and this file must remain a configuration that boots: an uncommented
# `TURNSTILE_SECRET=CHANGE_ME` would make the shipped example refuse to start, and
# `env.test.ts` asserts the example loads once the two real placeholders above are
# replaced. `TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES` is not optional ONCE the other two are set.
#
# ALL THREE, OR NONE. `src/env.ts` refuses the middle at boot, because both halves
# of a half-configured gate are silent: a secret with no site key refuses every
# registration (no browser can produce a token), and a site key with no secret
# renders a widget that is verified against nothing.
#
# WHAT EACH ONE IS:
#
#   TURNSTILE_SECRET     Issued by Cloudflare. A REAL SECRET — it is what proves a
#                        token to `siteverify`, and it never leaves this service.
#                        Vendor-shaped (`0x…`), so it is held to
#                        `assertOpaqueSecret` rather than the generated-secret
#                        rule the two keys above take: Cloudflare chose that
#                        alphabet, not this estate, and the strict rule would
#                        refuse a correct key at boot.
#
#   TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY   PUBLIC. It is served to browsers by `GET /auth/challenge`
#                        and appears in the page source of every site using
#                        Turnstile. It is a variable rather than a constant in
#                        hub-web because hub-web has NO build-time configuration —
#                        one image serves localhost, micro and mainnet.
#
#   TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES  Comma-separated bare hostnames — no scheme, no port, no
#                        path — that a solved widget may have been served from,
#                        compared against `siteverify`'s `hostname` field. This is
#                        not decoration: the site key is public, so without it a
#                        widget on an attacker's own page mints tokens this
#                        service accepts.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE and is what a developer machine, CI and every micro
# network run in: `GET /auth/challenge` answers `required: false`, hub-web renders
# no widget, and `POST /auth/register` behaves exactly as it did before
# micro-org#361.
#
# TURNSTILE_SECRET=
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# TURNSTILE_HOSTNAMES=hub.cloudsforge.online
